umbency, and a unanimous set of R-favorable expert ratings outweigh Barringer's solid fundraising and a national climate that appears friendlier to Democrats; markets price Knott's odds near 83 percent.", "watch": "Fresh district polling showing Barringer within single digits, or a further slide in Trump's standing in red North Carolina districts, could turn this into a genuine contest.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Brad Knott (R) is running."]}, "overview": "North Carolina's 13th District leans Republican by about eight points, and first-term incumbent Brad Knott is running for re-election with the advantages that come with it. Every listed race rater places the seat at Likely or Safe Republican, and prediction markets give Knott roughly an 83 percent chance of holding it. No public general-election polls are available, so the picture rests on the district's underlying partisanship, expert ratings, and fundraising.\n\nDemocrat Paul Barringer has run a serious campaign, raising just over one million dollars and ending the period within about $200,000 of Knott's cash on hand, and the national climate appears favorable to Democrats this year. Even so, the district's structural lean and Knott's incumbency set a high bar. A Libertarian nominee is on the ballot but is not a significant factor. The expected outcome is a Republican win by high single digits, with Barringer needing an unusually strong national wave to close the gap.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0835, "margin": -8.64}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0743, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1064, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.1381, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0942, "margin": -8.5}]}